@page "/async"

<PageTitle>Async rules</PageTitle>
<h1>Async rules</h1>
<p>Type "admin" and pause: each keystroke starts the check immediately (the delay simulates a server call — 600 ms by default, adjustable below), shows a pending state, and a new keystroke supersedes the in-flight check — its verdict is discarded, and on this page's memoized validator the call itself runs on so a retyped value can reuse its answer.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "Handle.cs", "MemoizedHandleValidator.cs", "AsyncRules.razor", "AsyncRules.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Username</strong>: checked for uniqueness asynchronously while you type (a delay stands in for the server — 600&nbsp;ms by default, adjustable below). Taken usernames: <code>admin</code>, <code>root</code>, <code>formidable</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Display name</strong>: checked for uniqueness the same way, independently of Username. Taken display names: <code>Administrator</code>, <code>Root User</code>, <code>Formidable</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Username</strong>: also required to submit, and the live channel answers that rule too: clear a username you have already typed and "Username is required" lands with no submit anywhere.</li>
            <li>Editing after a submit also schedules a background refresh (300&nbsp;ms) that reconciles the whole form — but the pending indicator stays on the field you edited, before and after submit alike.</li>
            <li>Editing after a submit still runs each check once. The refresh executes only the rules still owed an answer at the current edit, so a live pass that has already landed leaves it nothing to run, and where the two would overlap, the refresh stands down for a pass in flight and assembles from its answer. What the memo below catches is the caller the store cannot help — a Submit pressed while a check is still in flight joins the call in progress instead of starting a second.</li>
            <li>Both uniqueness checks also memoize their answer for 10&nbsp;seconds: type <code>admin</code>, clear it, then retype it, and the second check comes back from that memo instead of running again — the same memo that lets a Submit pressed shortly after a live pass skip a repeat round trip too.</li>
            <li><strong>Validator override</strong>: <code>FormidableForm</code>'s <code>Validator</code> parameter resolves from DI when left unset, which is what every other page here does; this page supplies <code>MemoizedHandleValidator</code> directly instead, which is why the memoizing shows up here rather than on <code>/field-state</code>.</li>
            <li>Each field's <code>checking…</code> sits in an element that renders from the first paint with its <code>role="status"</code> already on it; only the text inside comes and goes. A live region announces reliably when assistive technology was told about it before the content arrived, and a role that arrives in the same render as its own first message is the shape assistive technology is inconsistent about — the same reason <code>FormidableSummary</code> holds two fixed-role regions of its own.</li>
            <li><strong>Reading the form inline</strong>: <code>Context="formidable"</code> names the form's body, so markup inside it reads <code>formidable.Engine</code> directly, with nothing captured or injected to reach it — the line under the Submit button does exactly that. Reaching the engine and keeping the read current are separate jobs, though: an inline read is only as fresh as the form's last render, so this page also holds an <code>@@ref</code> to the form and subscribes to <code>Engine.StateChanged</code> through it. Keeping that subscription fed is the <code>@@ref</code>'s only job here; the read itself never touches it.</li>
            <li><strong>LiveDebounce</strong>: batches keystrokes. Tick the checkbox below and a burst of typing starts one check after you pause, instead of one per keystroke. It is a separate clock from the delay above — the delay sets how long a check takes once it starts; LiveDebounce sets when one starts. After a submit, the same edit also arms the refresh; whichever of the two runs first pays for the check, and the other reuses its answer instead of running it again — ticking the box costs nothing extra there either.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Type <code>admin</code> and pause — a spinner and "checking…" appear on that field only, then the taken message lands.</li>
            <li>Type quickly — each keystroke supersedes the in-flight check; only the final value gets a verdict.</li>
            <li>Type in Display name — its indicator runs independently; the pending state is scoped to the field being edited.</li>
            <li>Clear Username — "Username is required" appears as soon as that edit's live pass does, with no submit: you engaged the field, and the live channel runs the rules a submit would. The same pass answers Display name's rule as well, so if more than ten seconds have passed since you typed there, this edit waits on a fresh check of that value first. Submit anyway and the same rule blocks.</li>
            <li>Drag the delay to 2000 ms and type — the slider only changes how long the check itself takes, not when it starts or how often; watch the pending state hold for the full two seconds before the verdict lands.</li>
            <li>Submit, then type again — one "checking…" cycle, not two, even though editing after a submit also arms the background refresh (see above).</li>
            <li>Tick <em>Debounce live checks</em>, then type <code>formidable</code> fast — no "checking…" flashes mid-keystroke, and pausing lands one check rather than two. Editing after a submit arms the background refresh as well, and the pair share the answer rather than each paying for it, exactly as the <code>LiveDebounce</code> rule above describes.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<p>
    <label>Simulated delay
        <input type="range" min="0" max="2000" step="100" @bind="DelayMs" @bind:event="oninput" />
    </label>
    @HandleValidator.SimulatedDelayMs ms
</p>

<p>
    <label><input type="checkbox" @bind="LiveDebounceEnabled" /> Debounce live checks (batch fast typing into one pass)</label>
</p>

<FormidableForm Model="_handle" Options="_options" Validator="_validator" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid" @ref="_form" Context="formidable">
    <div class="summary-slot">
        <FormidableSummary />
    </div>

    <FormidableField For="() => _handle.Username" Context="field">
        <div class="field">
            <label>Username <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_handle.Username" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" /></label>
            <em role="status">@(field.State.IsValidating ? "checking…" : null)</em>
        </div>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _handle.Username" />
    </FormidableField>

    <FormidableField For="() => _handle.DisplayName" Context="field">
        <div class="field">
            <label>Display name <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_handle.DisplayName" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" /></label>
            <em role="status">@(field.State.IsValidating ? "checking…" : null)</em>
        </div>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _handle.DisplayName" />
    </FormidableField>

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </div>

    <p><small><code>Context="formidable"</code> names the form's own body, so this line reads <code>formidable.Engine.IsValidating</code> inline, with nothing captured or injected to reach it: <strong>@(formidable.Engine.IsValidating ? "a pass is in flight" : "no pass is in flight")</strong>. It answers for the whole form, where each field's own indicator above answers for that field alone.</small></p>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>

<p><small>The pending flag is scoped to the field being edited during a live pass — type in Username or Display name and only that field's "checking…" indicator shows, not both.</small></p>
